## Why Use Static Members?

Static members are useful when information must be shared
among all objects of a class.

A static member function does not have a this pointer.
It cannot directly access non-static data members because
it does not belong to one particular object.

## Array of Objects

BusTicket trip[4] creates four BusTicket objects.
Each object is initialized with a passenger name.

The range-based for loop visits and prints each ticket.

## Alternative Concept

A global variable could store the issued seat count, but
it would be less encapsulated.

A separate TicketOffice object could manage the counter.
That design is useful when multiple buses need independent
seat counts.

A single static counter is suitable when all tickets belong
to the same bus.

## Complexity Analysis

Time complexity:
- Creating one ticket: O(1)
- Checking and assigning a seat: O(1)
- Printing one ticket: O(1)
- Processing n tickets: O(n)

Space complexity:
- Shared counter: O(1)
- One ticket object: O(1)
- Array of n ticket objects: O(n)

